    chip(VINC-NEUTRO): round 5 — both gate sides REFUTED; make the impossible fixture unwriteable
    
    Round 5 ran as a delta gate over 394c83c..3915f33b, two seats on one frozen
    input each. Both returned REFUTED. Eight findings between them, all eight
    verified against primary source before any code moved.
    
    Two were code, and both are the same shape as a defect an earlier round
    declared closed:
    
    - VinculoDrawer kept a PRIVATE second copy of bandClasses, raw-indexed. Round 4
      hardened QueueRow's copy and a grep scoped to that file reported the class
      closed. The drawer's copy wrote the literal `undefined` into a class
      attribute for any band the wire ships before the SDK is regenerated. The copy
      is deleted; bandClass/bandLabel are exported from QueueRow, one reader per
      value for the whole screen.
    - providerDisplayName ran its injectivity round-trip on the string it had just
      built, not on what the browser paints. HTML collapses whitespace runs, so
      amazon_marketplace and amazon__marketplace still landed on one name. The
      check now runs on the painted form.
    
    Both were driven from captured red first.
    
    The other six were fixtures asserting a layout the backend cannot emit. That
    class had already recurred three times, so per the hub's third-occurrence rule
    this is a named mechanism plus an exhaustive re-sweep, not a sixth point-fix:
    
    - wireFixtures.ts makes an impossible candidate UNWRITEABLE rather than
      detectable. wireCandidate throws in the test that builds it, naming the
      generator line the fixture contradicts. driftCandidate(why, ...) is the
      explicit door for a shape that is unproducible ON PURPOSE, and it requires
      the reason to be written next to the fixture.
    - wireFixtures.guard.test.ts reads marketplace_capability.go and
      mercado_livre/capability_adapter.go at test time and asserts set equality
      with the FE's constants in both directions. This closes F-07, which this chip
      had filed as an accepted residual risk — which was, here, a way of deferring
      the work to the next reader. Must-failed both ways.
    
    The machine re-sweep returned 13 failed / 5 passed on first run against a
    document that called itself EXHAUSTIVE and had reported two findings in that
    same file. Six of the thirteen were a missing `marca` reason — the very
    invariant that sweep was built to enforce. The sweep's own exhaustiveness proof
    used a grep whose [a-z_]* class silently excluded every capitalized and
    accented anchor, reporting one violation where there were five.
    
    V-fixture-producibility-sweep.md is corrected at source per R-24: a banner at
    its head and a CORRECTION section naming each false sentence. Its verdicts are
    kept verbatim rather than edited into correctness, because what they got wrong
    is the finding.
    
    Two fixtures previously carried a comment claiming they were "producible under
    a capability declaration". The Opus seat called that a dodge rather than an
    honest degrade and it was right: resolveIdentityAnchors aborts generation
    unless the declaration resolves, so those reason arrays are unreachable under
